content.text:
The follow-up batch failed its sign-off on two tests, so it hasn't landed. Both failures came from one rule, which I've fixed, and the sign-off plus the desktop tests are running again.

- **What failed:** crawl has a rule that it may depend only on `playwright-core`. Follow-up A2 gave crawl Effect, to replace raw timers in its Appium waits.
- **Why I changed the rule rather than the code:** decision 0022 says Effect owns waits and timeouts. So I allowed Effect in crawl and wrote that reason next to the rule, the same way I did for review.
- **Checks on the fix:** both crawl rule tests and the lint baseline pass.
- **Desktop tests:** they passed in the failed run, so only the crawl rule needed fixing.

If this run passes, the controller branch moves to it. That brings in:
- Effect's own diagnostics in the checks;
- server raw timers down from 13 to 4;
- the plan's status update;
- the fix that keeps tabs and the desktop app attached when the server restarts Vite;
- coverage notices for runs replayed only inside a preview;
- the hidden-tab and frame test.

The follow-up batch has landed. It passed its full sign-off and every desktop test at `7c9a1442`, and the controller branch is now exactly that commit. That brings in:

- Effect's own checks;
- most server timers moved to Effect;
- the fix that keeps tabs and the desktop app attached when Vite restarts;
- coverage notices for runs replayed only inside a preview;
- the hidden-tab test;
- the fix for the flaky browser tests.

This was the first full browser run with the flaky-test fix, and it passed cleanly. That doesn't prove the fix yet, because the worker never got its three back-to-back full runs.

Only one piece is left: the worker adding the Effect rules from anti-slop, fixing up the rules and cleaning up `useEffect`. When it's back, I'll review it, give it one sign-off, and write the handover.

